    {:id :oilup/an-attested-gate-is-normalised-to-true
     :why "an attestation recorded as false must not be read as attested. The floor is gate-value's `or`, not missing-gates' `boolean` -- the `or` chain has already turned an explicit false into nil by the time missing-gates sees it, so mutating `boolean` to `some?` there would be vacuous"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "  (or (get attestations gate)\n      (get attestations (name gate))"
     :replace "  (or (when (some? (get attestations gate)) true)\n      (get attestations (name gate))"
     :must-fail ["false-attestation-does-not-satisfy-a-gate"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-blocked-plan-still-emits-its-effects
     :why "the one property this actor has: no :mst/put-record leaves the boundary unless every gate is attested. Returning the planned effects alongside :status :blocked is the plausible regression -- it reads like a debugging convenience and every downstream reader that dispatches on :effects rather than :status starts writing"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "         {:status :blocked\n          :effects []}"
     :replace "         {:status :blocked\n          :effects (mapv (fn [{:keys [collection record rkey]}]\n                           (put-record-effect collection rkey record))\n                         (records-for spec input))}"
     :must-fail ["blocked-plan-emits-no-effects"]}

    {:id :oilup/string-keyed-attestations-stop-resolving
     :why "callers hand attestations over as JSON, so string keys and string sets are the wire shape, not a convenience. Dropping the (name gate) lookup makes every such caller silently unattested -- the actor refuses to write and looks correct while doing it"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "\n      (get attestations (name gate))"
     :replace ""
     :must-fail ["gate-value-reads-all-four-attestation-shapes"
                 "a-plan-is-ready-when-attestations-arrive-as-a-set-of-strings"]}

    {:id :oilup/keyword-keyed-attestations-stop-resolving
     :why "the mirror of the above, and the clause that resolves a keyword set. Both of gate-value's reachable branches are pinned, because the two that follow them are dead"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "  (or (get attestations gate)\n"
     :replace "  (or\n"
     :must-fail ["gate-value-reads-all-four-attestation-shapes"]}

    {:id :oilup/the-string-rkey-fallback-is-dropped
     :why "`(get record* \"rkey\")` looks redundant next to `(:rkey record*)` and is the obvious thing to delete while tidying. It is the branch that JSON-shaped records land on, and losing it moves them to the request id -- so every record in one request collides on one key and put-record overwrites all but the last"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "\n                                 (get record* \"rkey\")"
     :replace ""
     :must-fail ["rkey-is-derived-in-a-fixed-order-of-preference"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-path-separator-survives-into-the-rkey
     :why "widening the safe set by one character is how sanitisers are relaxed -- someone wants hierarchical keys. A slash in an rkey escapes the collection it was addressed to"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~-]\" \"-\")"
     :replace "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~/-]\" \"-\")"
     :must-fail ["safe-rkey-replaces-every-character-outside-the-safe-set"]}

    {:id :oilup/rkey-collapse-repaired
     :why "hazard tripwire. safe-rkey turns every unsafe character into a single dash and asks str/blank? afterwards, so equal-length non-ASCII identifiers share an rkey and put-record overwrites. Collapsing runs is one plausible repair; either way the hazard test must notice and both it and this mutation should then be deleted"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~-]\" \"-\"))]\n    (if"
     :replace "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~-]+\" \"-\"))]\n    (if"
     :must-fail ["hazard-safe-rkey-collapses-distinct-identifiers-onto-one-key"]}

    {:id :oilup/record-merge-order-protects-the-stamp
     :why "hazard tripwire, and the actual repair: merging the caller's record BEFORE base makes :actorDid, :$type, :scaffold and :constitutionalStatus unspoofable in the record body. When this lands for real, delete the hazard test and this mutation"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(merge {:$type coll}\n                            base\n                            (or (get input-records coll)\n                                (get input-records idx)\n                                {}))"
     :replace "(merge {:$type coll}\n                            (or (get input-records coll)\n                                (get input-records idx)\n                                {})\n                            base)"
     :must-fail ["hazard-a-caller-supplied-record-overwrites-the-actor-stamp-in-the-body"]}

    {:id :oilup/the-planner-stamps-a-different-did
     :why "actor-did is the identity every effect is attributed under and the only thing tying this code to the manifest. Nothing in the repo cross-checked the two before this suite, so a drift here was invisible"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "  \"did:web:oil-upstream.etzhayyim.com\")"
     :replace "  \"did:web:oil-upstream.example.com\")"
     :must-fail ["manifest-and-cljc-boundary-name-the-same-actor"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-cell-is-renamed-away-from-its-endpoint
     :why "the manifest names endpoints in dotted NSID form and cell-specs in dashed legacy form; the only thing connecting them is that substitution. Renaming one side leaves an endpoint the actor serves with no cell that can answer it, and the manifest keeps advertising it"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find ":getfield {:legacy-cell \"com-etzhayyim-apps-oilUpstream-registry-getField\""
     :replace ":getfield {:legacy-cell \"com-etzhayyim-apps-oilUpstream-registry-getFieldV2\""
     :must-fail ["every-xrpc-endpoint-has-a-cell-that-can-serve-it"
                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/an-orphan-cell-is-added-without-a-manifest-obligation
     :why "the direction the one-directional coverage checks are blind to, and the reason the bijection exists: cells get added during migration and the manifest lags. A cell nobody asked for is reachable through all-cell-plans, writes to a collection under this actor's namespace, and no coverage check that walks the manifest can see it"
     :file "src/oil_upstream/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "  :koji {:legacy-cell \"koji\""
     :replace "  :orphan {:legacy-cell \"com-etzhayyim-apps-oilUpstream-orphan\"\n     :phase :event\n     :murakumo-node \"reuben\"\n     :collections [(collection \"orphan\")]\n     :required-gates common-gates\n     :trigger \"manifest cell orphan\"\n     :ceiling \"Manifest-driven migration scaffold; explicit execution stays in runtime methods\"}\n  :koji {:legacy-cell \"koji\""
     :must-fail ["the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"
                 "the-planner-still-has-something-to-plan"]}

    {:id :oilup/the-manifest-advertises-an-endpoint-with-no-cell
     :why "the same drift from the other side, which is the direction it actually happens: an endpoint is versioned in the manifest and the planner is not updated with it"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"nsid\": \"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilUpstream.registry.listFields\""
     :replace "\"nsid\": \"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilUpstream.registry.listFieldsV2\""
     :must-fail ["every-xrpc-endpoint-has-a-cell-that-can-serve-it"
                 "the-documented-xrpc-endpoints-are-still-the-ones-declared"
                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-subscription-arrives-with-no-cell-to-handle-it
     :why "the top-level triggers block is what a relay reads to decide what to deliver, so a collection listed there with no cell is a silent drop rather than an error. oilRefining.refinery arrives from a repo this one does not control"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilRefining.refinery\""
     :replace "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilRefining.crude\""
     :must-fail ["every-subscribed-collection-has-a-cell-that-can-handle-it"
                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-pipeline-is-triggered-by-a-collection-nobody-advertises
     :why "this manifest has two subscription surfaces that are allowed to differ in size -- the top-level block advertises five, the pipeline list runs steps for one. A pipeline keyed to a collection outside the advertised set never fires, and nothing about it looks wrong. The replacement here is a collection that DOES have a cell, so the coverage check stays green and only the subset check moves"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.oilMidstream.flow\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"subscribeRepos\""
     :replace "\"com.etzhayyim.apps.standard.shinkaEvolution\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"subscribeRepos\""
     :must-fail ["the-pipeline-subscriptions-are-a-subset-of-the-advertised-ones"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-required-loop-loses-the-cell-that-runs-it
     :why "requiredLoops is an obligation the standard rule places on this actor, and a loop with no cell simply never runs -- there is no error, the loop is just absent. Nothing in the repo connected the two before this suite"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\n    \"koji\",\n"
     :replace "\n    \"koji-v2\",\n"
     :must-fail ["every-required-loop-and-collection-has-a-cell"
                 "the-cell-catalogue-is-exactly-what-the-manifest-obliges"]}

    {:id :oilup/custom-escape-hatch-allowed
     :why "a step whose fn is outside the declared capability vocabulary runs with authority the manifest never granted. `custom` is the specific escape hatch the retired vitest file was written to refuse, so losing it would undo the one check that file was carried over for"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"fn\": \"derive:social\""
     :replace "\"fn\": \"custom\""
     :must-fail ["no-pipeline-step-escapes-into-a-custom-function"]}

    {:id :oilup/a-step-reaches-for-a-capability-the-actor-never-declared
     :why "the vitest file only checked steps against the global vocabulary, so a step could use any known capability whether or not THIS actor declared it. graph.vectorSearch is in the vocabulary and not in this manifest's capabilities list -- the check that catches it is the per-actor one, and only that one moves here"
     :file "actor-manifest.jsonld"
     :find "\"fn\": \"graph.write\""
     :replace "\"fn\": \"graph.vectorSearch\""
     :must-fail ["every-pipeline-step-fn-is-a-capability-the-manifest-declared"]}

    {:id :oilup/served-did-document-is-reconciled
     :why "hazard tripwire. .well-known/did.json identifies itself as did:web:etzhayyim.com:actor:oil-upstream while the planner and the manifest both stamp did:web:oil-upstream.etzhayyim.com, and alsoKnownAs bridges neither. Which side moves is an ownership decision; this fires whichever side moves first"
     :file ".well-known/did.json"
     :find "\"id\" : \"did:web:etzhayyim.com:actor:oil-upstream\","
     :replace "\"id\" : \"did:web:oil-upstream.etzhayyim.com\","
     :must-fail ["hazard-the-served-did-document-does-not-claim-the-did-this-actor-stamps"]}

    {:id :oilup/the-evidence-floor-itself
     :why "the floor under the suite, guarded like anything else. Dropping a namespace from the runner leaves a run that prints `0 failures, 0 errors` and would otherwise be read as a pass -- the exact shape ADR-2608136000 is about. It must exit 2, not 0 and not 1, and it must not print the marker. `must-fail` names the REFUSED line rather than a test, because the point is that no test ran"
     :file "run_tests.cljs"
     :find "(t/run-tests 'oil_upstream.murakumo-test\n             'oil_upstream.manifest-test)"
     :replace "(t/run-tests 'oil_upstream.manifest-test)"
     :must-fail ["REFUSED"]}]}
  {:repo "orgs/cloud-itonami/gov-municipality"
   :label "gov-municipality permitting actor: the gate, the clock, and the six files that describe one actor"
   ;; Until 2026-08-31 this repo's suite was green exactly one way: `run_tests.sh`
   ;; built a mktemp symlink farm to reconcile namespaces with paths that did not
   ;; match them, and drove four namespaces through `bb` -- a retired script host
   ;; here (ADR-2607173000), reached through a .sh file, which is not an authoring
   ;; surface either. `methods/agent.cljc` was .cljc in extension only: it read
   ;; `System/currentTimeMillis` and referred to `clojure.string/split` without
   ;; requiring it, so Babashka's preload was the only thing holding it up.
   ;; `src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc` -- the fail-closed gate -- was in none
   ;; of the four namespaces and had never been executed on any runtime.
   ;;
   ;; All ten below were replayed by hand at 9c5827be on 2026-08-31, one at a
   ;; time with the file restored between runs, before any of them was written
   ;; here. Every anchor was checked to occur exactly once; base green before and
   ;; after; no mutation printed the green marker. The runner's three exit codes
   ;; were each shown to discriminate rather than assumed: breaking
   ;; `missing-gates` gives exit 1 with 28 failures and no marker, dropping a
   ;; namespace from `run-tests` gives exit 2 while printing "0 failures,
   ;; 0 errors", and the unmodified tree gives exit 0 with the marker. The last
   ;; is registered below, so the floor is guarded the same way the code is.
   :cmd ["nbb" "--classpath" "src:test" "run_tests.cljs"]
   :green-marker "gov-municipality: all green"
   :mutations
   [{:id :gov/gate-check-removed
     :why "the invariant the boundary exists for. With nothing removed from :required-gates, `missing-gates` returns empty for every input and every cell plans as :ready -- an actor that writes to the MST without a single attestation. This is the shape the drift takes if the gate is ever 'simplified'"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(remove #(boolean (gate-value attestations %)))"
     :replace "(remove (fn [_] true))"
     :must-fail ["blocked-plan-emits-no-effects"]}

    {:id :gov/presence-counts-as-attestation
     :why "a gate recorded as `false` is a refusal, not a signature. `contains?` is the plausible-looking edit -- it reads as 'the attestation is present' -- and it turns every explicit `false` into a pass. Nothing else in the suite distinguishes an absent gate from one that was actively denied"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "  (or (get attestations gate)"
     :replace "  (or (and (map? attestations) (contains? attestations gate))"
     :must-fail ["falsey-attestation-is-a-refusal-not-a-signature"]}

    {:id :gov/path-separator-survives-into-rkey
     :why "`safe-rkey` decides what a record is called in the MST. Widening the safe alphabet by one character lets a slash through, and an rkey containing a path separator is not a key -- it is a path. The DIDs this actor derives rkeys from are full of colons and dots, so the alphabet is load-bearing"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/murakumo.cljc"
     :find "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~-]\" \"-\")"
     :replace "(str/replace #\"[^A-Za-z0-9._~/-]\" \"-\")"
     :must-fail ["safe-rkey-strips-did-web-and-unsafe-characters"]}

    {:id :gov/clock-goes-back-to-jvm-only
     :why "the exact regression this commit removed. `System/currentTimeMillis` in a .cljc file is invisible on the JVM -- it is the runtime it works on -- so only a run that is not the JVM can see it. nbb refuses at analysis, so :must-fail names the refusal rather than a test: the namespace never loads, and 'the suite went red' would otherwise count any unrelated breakage as this one"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/methods/agent.cljc"
     :find "#?(:clj (System/currentTimeMillis)\n     :cljs (.now js/Date))"
     :replace "(System/currentTimeMillis)"
     :must-fail ["Unable to resolve symbol: System/currentTimeMillis"]}

    {:id :gov/clojure-string-require-dropped
     :why "the other half of that regression. The file referred to `clojure.string/split` for months without requiring the namespace and nobody noticed, because Babashka preloads it. Dropping the require again is silent on bb and refused by nbb at the first `str/` call"
     :file "src/gov_municipality/methods/agent.cljc"
     :find "  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))"
     :replace "  )"
     :must-fail ["Unable to resolve symbol: str/starts-with?"]}

    {:id :gov/app-manifest-src-drifts
     :why "kotoba.app.edn names the three files `kotoba app deploy` compiles. It fell behind the tree during this very commit -- the cells moved and the manifest did not follow -- and the test caught it. This is that drift, replayed"
     :file "kotoba.app.edn"
     :find "\"src/gov_municipality/cells/final_sign_off/state_machine.cljc\""
     :replace "\"cells/final_sign_off/state_machine.cljc\""
     :must-fail ["kotoba-app-src-paths-exist"]}

    {:id :gov/cell-cites-an-undeclared-gate
     :why "a cell sidecar claiming to enforce a gate the actor does not declare. The gate ids are the actor's constitutional surface (manifest.edn :actor/gates); a cell citing G21 is either a typo or a gate somebody removed from the manifest without following it into the cells"
     :file "cells/permit_submission.edn"
     :find ":cell/gates [\"G1\" \"G15\" \"G16\" \"G19\" \"G20\"]"
     :replace ":cell/gates [\"G1\" \"G15\" \"G16\" \"G19\" \"G21\"]"
     :must-fail ["cells-only-cite-gates-the-manifest-declares"]}

    {:id :gov/lex-record-leaves-the-shared-root
     :why "the four lexicon records must agree on one root, whatever it is. This repo already spells its lexicon root three different ways across prose, cljc and lex/*.edn -- that divergence is pinned by a separate test. What must not happen on top of it is the lex files themselves splitting"
     :file "lex/inspectionSchedule.edn"
     :find ":lex/id \"com.etzhayyim.govmunicipality.inspectionSchedule\""
     :replace ":lex/id \"com.etzhayyim.gov.inspectionSchedule\""
     :must-fail ["lex-records-share-one-root-and-end-with-their-declared-name"]}

    {:id :gov/manifest-cell-loses-its-sidecar
     :why "manifest.edn and cells/*.edn are two lists of the same cells. Renaming one entry leaves a declared cell with no sidecar and a sidecar no manifest entry -- checked in both directions, because only one direction catches a rename"
     :file "manifest.edn"
     :find "{:cell/id \"ledger\"                :cell/kind :datalog   :cell/runtime :kotoba}"
     :replace "{:cell/id \"audit\"                 :cell/kind :datalog   :cell/runtime :kotoba}"
     :must-fail ["every-manifest-cell-has-a-sidecar-and-every-sidecar-a-manifest-entry"]}

    {:id :gov/namespace-dropped-from-the-runner
     :why "the floor, guarded like the code. A namespace quietly dropped from `run-tests` prints '0 failures, 0 errors' -- the same words a full green run prints -- so without the floor a suite that stopped measuring the boundary reads as a suite that found nothing wrong. It must exit 2, not 0 and not 1, and must not print the marker. :must-fail names the REFUSED line rather than a test, because the point is that the tests did not run"
     :file "run_tests.cljs"
     :find "             'gov_municipality.murakumo-test\n             'gov_municipality.manifest-test)"
     :replace "             'gov_municipality.manifest-test)"
     :must-fail ["REFUSED"]}]}

  {:repo "orgs/kotoba-lang/playwright"
   :label "playwright marshalling seams and eval-page's three-way dispatch"
   ;; Until 2026-08-31 the only test that could run here without a browser was
   ;; `(is (boolean? (pw/available?)))` -- true whichever way available? answers,
   ;; and true as well if it had been a constant. The suite was green and measured
   ;; nothing. The eight below were each replayed at pin b377e887 before landing:
   ;; anchor unique, base green before and after, suite red naming that test.
   ;;
   ;; Two of them are the CLAUDE.md floor rather than ordinary correctness --
   ;; :error-swallowed and :bridge-failure-returns-nil turn a failure into nil,
   ;; which is exactly what a successful eval of an expression with no value
   ;; returns. A browser error and a harness that was never installed would then
   ;; be indistinguishable from a page that answered.
   ;;
   ;; NOT covered here: everything reached through a real browser. `clojure -M:test`
   ;; (the smoke suite) needs an installed chromium and is red on any machine
   ;; without one, so it is not the suite this loop runs.
   :cmd ["clojure" "-M:kotoba-test"]
   :green-marker "0 failures, 0 errors"
   :mutations
   [{:id :playwright/clj-conversion-stops-recursing
     :why "the shape this actually regresses in -- somebody reads the reduce as a plain walk and drops the recursive call. Clojure's = considers a java.util.HashMap equal to a Clojure map, so an =-only test stays green here; the test asserts map?/vector? on a map nested inside a list, which is what discriminates"
     :file "src/playwright_clj/core.clj"
     :find "(assoc! m (key e) (->clj (val e)))"
     :replace "(assoc! m (key e) (val e))"
     :must-fail ["->clj-recurses-into-nested-java-collections"]}

    {:id :playwright/cdp-key-keeps-its-colon
     :why "`name` -> `str` on CDP parameter keys. CDP ignores unknown parameters silently, so the virtual authenticator comes up with defaults and the passkey ceremony fails far from the cause"
     :file "src/playwright_clj/core.clj"
     :find "(doseq [[k v] x] (.add o (name k) (clj->gson v))) o)"
     :replace "(doseq [[k v] x] (.add o (str k) (clj->gson v))) o)"
     :must-fail ["cdp-keyword-keys-lose-the-colon"]}

    {:id :playwright/cdp-value-keeps-its-colon
     :why "the same slip on the value side, which is how it actually shipped -- keys used `name`, values fell through to `str`, so {:transport :internal} sent \":internal\". Removing the branch restores the bug this test was written from"
     :file "src/playwright_clj/core.clj"
     :find "    (keyword? x) (com.google.gson.JsonPrimitive. ^String (name x))\n"
     :replace ""
     :must-fail ["cdp-keyword-values-lose-the-colon"]}

    {:id :playwright/cdp-booleans-become-strings
     :why "a redundant-looking branch removed in a tidy-up. true then falls to `str` and CDP receives the string \"true\", which is not a boolean anywhere in the protocol"
     :file "src/playwright_clj/core.clj"
     :find "    (boolean? x) (com.google.gson.JsonPrimitive. ^Boolean (boolean x))\n"
     :replace ""
     :must-fail ["cdp-booleans-stay-json-booleans"]}

    {:id :playwright/error-swallowed
     :why "FLOOR. An ok:false response degraded to (:result parsed) -- nil. Every failed browser eval then returns exactly what a successful eval of a valueless expression returns, and the caller cannot tell that the browser reported an error at all"
     :file "src/kami/playwright.clj"
     :find "          parsed (throw (ex-info (str \"browser eval error: \" (:error parsed)) {}))"
     :replace "          parsed (:result parsed)"
     :must-fail ["eval-page-throws-when-the-browser-reports-an-error"]}

    {:id :playwright/bridge-failure-returns-nil
     :why "FLOOR, the other half. Unparseable stdout means the bridge never ran -- no Playwright package, no node module, a crash. Returning nil makes 'the harness is absent' indistinguishable from 'the page answered nothing'"
     :file "src/kami/playwright.clj"
     :find "          :else  (throw (ex-info (str \"playwright bridge failed (exit \" exit \")\") {:err err :out out}))"
     :replace "          :else  nil"
     :must-fail ["eval-page-throws-when-the-bridge-never-ran"]}

    {:id :playwright/available-stops-answering
     :why "available? is a guard callers wrap browser-dependent work in; if it propagates instead of answering, every consumer that asked whether it could proceed dies at the question"
     :file "src/kami/playwright.clj"
     :find "       (catch Exception _ false)))"
     :replace "       (catch Exception e (throw e))))"
     :must-fail ["available?-returns-false-rather-than-throwing-when-the-bridge-is-broken"]}

    {:id :playwright/facade-drifts-from-the-ssot
     :why "ADR-2607102200 addendum 8 makes kami.playwright the SSoT and kotoba.playwright a re-export. A facade that stops being the same var is a second implementation, and the old boolean? test passed on exactly this"
     :file "src/kotoba/playwright.clj"
     :find "(def available? impl/available?)"
     :replace "(def available? (constantly true))"
     :must-fail ["facade-re-exports-the-same-fns"]}]}

  {:repo "orgs/kotoba-lang/logistics"
   :label "logistics: the tracking contract, the two export surfaces, and the console that must stay read-only"
   ;; Before this, the suite was 11 tests / 27 assertions, and every export test
   ;; was a `re-find` over the emitted text. A substring match cannot see a row
   ;; that stopped having as many fields as its header, a quote that was opened
   ;; and never closed, or a control character that makes the document
   ;; unreadable to every conforming parser -- it only reports that some bytes
   ;; it already knew about are somewhere in the output. So the export tests now
   ;; decode, with `kotoba.logistics.readback`: an RFC 4180 reader and an RFC
   ;; 8259 reader written from the grammars and pinned, fixture by fixture,
   ;; against Python's `csv` and `json` modules. The oracle has its own tests,
   ;; because a decoder written from the encoder agrees with it by construction.
   ;;
   ;; All 29 were replayed by hand at pin 599c8ee on 2026-08-31 before landing,
   ;; one at a time with the file restored between runs and the tree confirmed
   ;; clean afterwards: every anchor unique, every bite naming the test that
   ;; claims the invariant, base green before the battery and after it.
   ;;
   ;; Two of the 29 run under nbb rather than the JVM, and neither is visible to
   ;; `clojure -M:test`. `kotoba.logistics` says of itself that it is portable
   ;; across JVM / ClojureScript / SCI / GraalVM and nothing had ever run it
   ;; anywhere but the JVM; `json-hex4` carries a comment saying it avoids
   ;; Integer interop "that would only work on :clj" and nothing checked that
   ;; either. This is the shape run.cljs already records: a `#?(:clj int :cljs
   ;; identity)` mutation scored 0 failures under `clojure -M:test` and 11 under
   ;; nbb. `kotoba.logistics.ui` is not in the nbb run -- it needs html/css as
   ;; git deps, and a runner that cannot start reports what a runner that found
   ;; nothing wrong reports.
   ;;
   ;; The nbb runner's three exit codes were each shown to discriminate rather
   ;; than assumed: dropping a namespace from its list gives exit 2 while still
   ;; printing "0 failures, 0 errors" and no marker; breaking an invariant gives
   ;; exit 1 with the failing test named; the unmodified tree gives 0 with the
   ;; marker. The first version of the floor was set from the JVM totals by
   ;; mistake and refused its own green run, which is the floor working.
   :cmd ["clojure" "-M:test"]
   :green-marker "0 failures, 0 errors"
   :mutations
   [{:id :logistics/tracking-matched-as-a-substring
     :why "`re-matches` anchors and `re-find` does not, and they are one word apart in the same form. With `re-find`, a mis-keyed number with the carrier's punctuation still attached passes, and so does a 60-character paste -- each contains a run of eight or more upper alphanumerics somewhere inside it. The old suite only tried well-formed and too-short inputs, so nothing stood between the two spellings"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "(boolean (re-matches #\"[A-Z0-9]{8,35}\" t))"
     :replace "(boolean (re-find #\"[A-Z0-9]{8,35}\" t))"
     :must-fail ["a-tracking-number-must-match-as-a-whole-not-as-a-substring"]}

    {:id :logistics/tracking-ceiling-removed
     :why "the upper bound of the declared 8..35 interval. The old suite tested 5 and 16 characters, so both ends could move in either direction unobserved -- and a validator with no ceiling accepts a whole pasted email as a tracking number"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "re-matches #\"[A-Z0-9]{8,35}\" t"
     :replace "re-matches #\"[A-Z0-9]{8,}\" t"
     :must-fail ["the-accepted-tracking-length-is-a-closed-interval"]}

    {:id :logistics/tracking-floor-lowered
     :why "the lower bound of the same interval. A floor of 1 accepts every stray keystroke in the tracking column as a valid number, and the settlement file then reports it as validated"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "re-matches #\"[A-Z0-9]{8,35}\" t"
     :replace "re-matches #\"[A-Z0-9]{1,35}\" t"
     :must-fail ["the-accepted-tracking-length-is-a-closed-interval"]}

    {:id :logistics/normalize-drops-case-folding
     :why "normalization is case folding AND separator stripping; dropping either half leaves a function that still returns a plausible string for every input. A lower-case tracking number then fails validation while the operator's screen shows it exactly as the carrier printed it"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "(str/upper-case (str/replace s #\"[ \\-]\" \"\"))"
     :replace "(str/replace s #\"[ \\-]\" \"\")"
     :must-fail ["normalization-folds-case-and-strips-the-two-separators-carriers-print"]}

    {:id :logistics/normalize-keeps-hyphens
     :why "the other half. Carriers print both separators; keeping hyphens means `1Z-999-AA1-0123` is rejected as malformed rather than normalized, which reads as a carrier problem rather than a code one"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "#\"[ \\-]\""
     :replace "#\"[ ]\""
     :must-fail ["normalization-folds-case-and-strips-the-two-separators-carriers-print"]}

    {:id :logistics/status-set-loses-exception
     :why "the constructor answers nil for a status it does not know, which is the same answer it gives for a genuinely bad one. Narrowing the set therefore does not raise anything -- the exception shipment simply stops existing, and every downstream count is quietly right about a smaller world"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "#{:booked :picked-up :in-transit :delivered :exception}"
     :replace "#{:booked :picked-up :in-transit :delivered}"
     :must-fail ["shipment-accepts-every-declared-status"]}

    {:id :logistics/mode-set-loses-sea
     :why "same shape for route legs. A sea leg becomes nil, the route still has a length, and the consignment CSV still reports a leg count -- one short"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "#{:road :rail :air :sea}"
     :replace "#{:road :rail :air}"
     :must-fail ["leg-accepts-every-declared-mode"]}

    {:id :logistics/validate-forgets-the-normalized-form
     :why "a valid result must carry the normalized number. Dropping it leaves `:logistics/valid? true` with a nil normalized form, so both exporters publish an empty normalized column for a number they have just called valid -- and the caller that reads the map without checking the flag gets nil instead of a half-normalized string"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "{:logistics/valid? true :logistics/normalized (normalize-tracking s)}"
     :replace "{:logistics/valid? true}"
     :must-fail ["a-validation-result-carries-an-error-or-a-normalized-form-never-both"]}

    {:id :logistics/validate-accepts-a-non-string
     :why "`tracking-valid?` and `validate-tracking` answer the same question through different code, and different callers ask different ones -- the console asks one, the exporters ask the other. A change that reaches only one of them splits the operator's screen from the operator's file, and neither looks wrong on its own"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "(not (string? s))         {:logistics/valid? false :logistics/error :not-a-string}"
     :replace "(not (string? s))         {:logistics/valid? true :logistics/error :not-a-string}"
     :must-fail ["the-two-tracking-entry-points-reach-the-same-verdict"]}

    {:id :logistics/consignment-invents-its-own-status
     :why "a consignment reports the shipment's status rather than having one. A default here shows an exception shipment as booked on the consignment line of the same console, and the two lines disagree without either being obviously wrong"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find ":cons/status     (:shipment/status shipment-record)"
     :replace ":cons/status     :booked"
     :must-fail ["a-consignment-takes-its-status-from-the-shipment-it-carries"]}

    {:id :logistics/delivered-predicate-also-answers-for-in-transit
     :why "the two predicates partition the statuses they name. Widening one so it answers for a neighbour is the plausible edit -- somebody wants `delivered?` to mean 'no longer our problem' -- and every existing assertion about a delivered shipment still holds"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find "(defn delivered? [s] (= :delivered (:shipment/status s)))"
     :replace "(defn delivered? [s] (contains? #{:delivered :in-transit} (:shipment/status s)))"
     :must-fail ["the-status-predicates-answer-only-for-their-own-status"]}

    {:id :logistics/a-route-becomes-a-set
     :why "order is the route. A set has the right leg count, so the console's Legs column and the consignment CSV both stay correct while Tokyo->Nagoya->Osaka silently becomes an unordered bag of legs"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics.cljc"
     :find ":cons/route      legs"
     :replace ":cons/route      (set legs)"
     :must-fail ["a-route-keeps-its-legs-in-the-order-they-were-given"]}

    {:id :export/csv-stops-quoting-a-bare-cr
     :why "RFC 4180 needs CR quoted as much as LF, and every standard reader treats a lone CR as a record boundary -- Python's splits the row into two. This is the regression the file's own comment says was there before; the mutation is a revert to it"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "#\"[\\\",\\n\\r]\""
     :replace "#\"[\\\",\\n]\""
     :must-fail ["every-csv-column-survives-a-round-trip-through-an-rfc4180-reader"]}

    {:id :export/csv-stops-quoting-the-separator
     :why "a warehouse called `Store B, Annex` then becomes two fields. The row still parses, so a reader that only checks for expected substrings finds everything it was looking for -- the failure is only visible to something that counts the fields the reader produced"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "#\"[\\\",\\n\\r]\""
     :replace "#\"[\\\"\\n\\r]\""
     :must-fail ["every-csv-export-row-has-as-many-fields-as-its-header"]}

    {:id :export/csv-stops-doubling-an-embedded-quote
     :why "RFC 4180 section 2.7: a quote inside a quoted field is written twice. Nothing covered this before -- the field is still wrapped in quotes, so the document looks right, and a carrier called `Carrier \\\"Fast\\\" Ltd` ends the field early and shifts every column after it"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(str \"\\\"\" (str/replace s \"\\\"\" \"\\\"\\\"\") \"\\\"\")"
     :replace "(str \"\\\"\" s \"\\\"\")"
     :must-fail ["every-csv-column-survives-a-round-trip-through-an-rfc4180-reader"]}

    {:id :export/csv-header-gains-a-column-the-rows-do-not
     :why "adding a column to the header and forgetting the row is the ordinary way a CSV export breaks. Every field keeps its own value and every substring assertion keeps passing; only the arity against the header moves"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "[\"shipment_id\" \"origin\" \"destination\" \"carrier\" \"tracking\" \"status\"]"
     :replace "[\"shipment_id\" \"origin\" \"destination\" \"carrier\" \"tracking\" \"weight\" \"status\"]"
     :must-fail ["every-csv-export-row-has-as-many-fields-as-its-header"]}

    {:id :export/csv-column-is-renamed
     :why "downstream settlement importers key on these strings, and the contract is not written down anywhere else. `legs` to `leg_count` is an improvement to read and a breaking change to consume"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "\"consignment_id\" \"shipment\" \"legs\" \"declared_value\" \"status\""
     :replace "\"consignment_id\" \"shipment\" \"leg_count\" \"declared_value\" \"status\""
     :must-fail ["csv-headers-are-the-published-column-names"]}

    {:id :export/consignment-legs-column-carries-the-route
     :why "the column is a count. Putting the route there produces a printed collection full of commas -- which `csv-cell` then quotes correctly, so the row still parses with the right arity and only the value is wrong"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(count (:cons/route c))"
     :replace "(:cons/route c)"
     :must-fail ["the-consignment-legs-column-is-a-count-not-the-route"]}

    {:id :export/json-stops-escaping-the-top-of-the-c0-range
     :why "RFC 8259 section 7 requires all of U+0000-U+001F escaped. Narrowing the range leaves the common escapes (tab, LF, CR) working, so the export still looks right for every value anyone tests by hand -- and a field carrying U+001F produces a document Python's json module refuses to read"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(for [i (range 0x20)]"
     :replace "(for [i (range 0x0a)]"
     :must-fail ["the-json-export-is-readable-for-every-c0-control-character"]}

    {:id :export/json-hex-digits-come-out-reversed
     :why "the shift order in `json-hex4`. Reversed, U+0001 is written as \\u1000 -- still four hex digits, still valid JSON, still parses. The value is simply a different character, which no structural check on the document would notice"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(for [shift [12 8 4 0]]"
     :replace "(for [shift [0 4 8 12]]"
     :must-fail ["the-json-export-is-readable-for-every-c0-control-character"]}

    {:id :export/json-stops-escaping-at-all
     :why "the floor under every JSON field. Removing `str/escape` leaves `json-string-escapes` and `json-hex4` in the file, fully written and never called, which is how this kind of change survives review"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(str/escape (str (if (nil? v) \"\" v)) json-string-escapes)"
     :replace "(str (if (nil? v) \"\" v))"
     :must-fail ["every-json-member-survives-a-round-trip-through-an-rfc8259-reader"]}

    {:id :export/json-tracking-verdict-is-always-true
     :why "the CSV and the JSON are two renderings of one call to `validate-tracking`, and each spells the verdict differently -- \"yes\"/\"no\" against true/false. Nothing made them stay in agreement, so a change to one is invisible to a test that reads the other"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "\"\\\"valid\\\":\" (if (:logistics/valid? r) \"true\" \"false\") \",\""
     :replace "\"\\\"valid\\\":\" \"true\" \",\""
     :must-fail ["the-csv-and-json-tracking-exports-decide-the-same-way"]}

    {:id :ui/tracking-column-uses-raw-hiccup
     :why "`html.core` escapes text; `[:hiccup/raw ...]` does not, and the stylesheet already goes through it in the same file, so a second use reads as ordinary. The page still renders and the column still shows the right text -- the console has just become an injection surface for anyone who can name a carrier"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/ui.cljc"
     :find "[:td (str t)]"
     :replace "[:td [:hiccup/raw (str t)]]"
     :must-fail ["no-operator-supplied-value-reaches-the-document-unescaped"]}

    {:id :ui/carrier-column-uses-raw-hiccup
     :why "the same edit one column over, registered separately because the test walks every column an operator-supplied string can reach and a test that only covered the first one would look identical from outside"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/ui.cljc"
     :find "[:td (:shipment/carrier s)]"
     :replace "[:td [:hiccup/raw (str (:shipment/carrier s))]]"
     :must-fail ["no-operator-supplied-value-reaches-the-document-unescaped"]}

    {:id :ui/console-gains-a-dispatch-button
     :why "the console observes; the governor decides. A control arrives looking like an improvement, and the old test named only `<form` and `<button` -- an `<input`, an `onclick`, or a `<dialog` would have walked straight past it"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/ui.cljc"
     :find "[:span.badge \"read-only · governor-gated\"]"
     :replace "[:button \"Dispatch\"]"
     :must-fail ["the-console-renders-no-write-surface-at-all"]}

    {:id :ui/a-status-loses-its-badge
     :why "three statuses get a colour and the rest fall through to muted. The fall-through is what breaks quietly: an exception shipment still renders, still says \"exception\", and is no longer red"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/ui.cljc"
     :find "(= :exception (:shipment/status s)) [:span.err \"exception\"]"
     :replace "(= :cancelled (:shipment/status s)) [:span.err \"exception\"]"
     :must-fail ["every-shipment-status-is-shown-with-its-own-badge"]}

    {:id :ui/an-empty-section-still-renders
     :why "a section with no records must not claim one. An always-rendered `Tracking validation` heading over an empty table tells an operator that nothing needs checking, which is the same thing it would say if the data never arrived"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/ui.cljc"
     :find "(when (seq trackings)"
     :replace "(when true"
     :must-fail ["a-section-appears-only-when-it-has-records"]}

    ;; ── the two the JVM run cannot see ──────────────────────────────────────
    {:id :export/json-hex-cljs-branch-drops-a-digit
     :why "a reader conditional whose :cljs branch is wrong. `clojure -M:test` takes the :clj branch and reports 0 failures for all thirty-two control characters; under nbb the top half of the C0 range comes out as \\u000X instead of \\u001X. This is the exact shape run.cljs already records for `#?(:clj int :cljs identity)` -- 0 failures on the JVM, 11 under nbb -- and the reason this repo now has a second runner at all"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(apply str (for [shift [12 8 4 0]] (nth json-hex-digits (bit-and (bit-shift-right n shift) 0xf))))"
     :replace "#?(:clj (apply str (for [shift [12 8 4 0]] (nth json-hex-digits (bit-and (bit-shift-right n shift) 0xf)))) :cljs (str \"000\" (nth json-hex-digits (bit-and n 0xf))))"
     :cmd ["nbb" "--classpath" "src:test" "run_tests.cljs"]
     :green-marker "logistics: all green"
     :must-fail ["the-json-export-is-readable-for-every-c0-control-character"]}

    {:id :export/json-hex-reaches-for-jvm-interop
     :why "`json-hex4`'s docstring says it uses bit ops and a lookup table specifically to avoid \"Long/Integer interop that would only work on :clj\". Nothing checked that claim, and the JVM run structurally cannot -- it is the runtime the interop works on. nbb refuses at analysis, so the :must-fail names the refusal itself rather than a test: the namespace never loads, and 'the suite went red' would otherwise count any unrelated breakage as this one"
     :file "src/kotoba/logistics/export.cljc"
     :find "(apply str (for [shift [12 8 4 0]] (nth json-hex-digits (bit-and (bit-shift-right n shift) 0xf))))"
     :replace "(let [h (Integer/toHexString n)] (str (subs \"0000\" 0 (- 4 (count h))) h))"
     :cmd ["nbb" "--classpath" "src:test" "run_tests.cljs"]
     :green-marker "logistics: all green"
     :must-fail ["Unable to resolve symbol: Integer/toHexString"]}

    {:id :logistics/a-namespace-is-dropped-from-the-portable-runner
     :why "the floor under the portable runner, guarded like the code. A namespace quietly dropped from `run-tests` prints '0 failures, 0 errors' -- the same words a full green run prints -- so without the floor a suite that stopped measuring a third of itself reads as a suite that found nothing wrong. It must exit 2, not 0 and not 1, and must not print the marker. `must-fail` names the REFUSED line rather than a test, because the point is that the tests did not run"
     :file "run_tests.cljs"
     :find "(t/run-tests 'kotoba.logistics-test\n             'kotoba.logistics.export-test\n             'kotoba.logistics.readback-test)"
     :replace "(t/run-tests 'kotoba.logistics-test\n             'kotoba.logistics.export-test)"
     :cmd ["nbb" "--classpath" "src:test" "run_tests.cljs"]
     :green-marker "logistics: all green"
     :must-fail ["REFUSED"]}]}
    {:repo "orgs/cloud-itonami/app-tenki"
     :label "app-tenki appview wiring —— ビルド設定・配信文書・マウント先を繋ぐ文字列"
     ;; パッケージ内の cljs.test suite は `tenki.app` **1 名前空間の中**を検査する。
     ;; ビルド設定と、配信される文書と、そこへマウントするコードを繋いでいる文字列は
     ;; 2 つのファイルに重複して書かれていて、誰も突き合わせていない。
     ;;
     ;; どの継ぎ目も壊れ方が同じである: 片側を変えると `shadow-cljs compile app` は通り、
     ;; `node out/tests.js` も `0 failures, 0 errors` を出し、**画面には何も出ない**。
     ;; CLAUDE.md が名指ししている形——測れなかった検査が、測って問題が無かった検査と
     ;; 同じ値を返す——の UI 版で、しかもこの repo は既に一度それを踏んでいる
     ;; （旧 Svelte 版の `test/tenki.test.ts` は `expect(true).toBe(true)` だった）。
     ;;
     ;; 9 件とも pin 80b2ea97 で landing 前に赤くなるところを実測した。7 件は exit 1 で
     ;; 自分の不変条件名を出し、後ろ 2 件は **exit 2**（REFUSED）を出す。0 でも 1 でも
     ;; ない値でなければ、「入力が読めなかった」が「読んで問題が無かった」と同じ顔をする。
     ;;
     ;; ここに無いもの: `:wiring/exactly-one-document` の mutation。これは
     ;; **文書をもう 1 枚足す**ことでしか壊せず、この harness は find/replace しか
     ;; 持たない。手では確認してある（2 枚目の html を置くと exit 1 + その名前）。
     ;; 同じ理由で `test/` を空にする refusal も入っていない。
     :cmd ["nbb" "test/appview_wiring_test.cljs"]
     :green-marker "appview-wiring: OK"
     :mutations
     [{:id :wiring/asset-path-goes-absolute
       :why "docs/operator-quickstart.md は asset-path を相対にしてあるのは意図だと記録している——これらのページは path prefix 配下で配信されるので、絶対 \"/js\" はルート直下でだけ動いて他で 404 する。ローカルでは常に動くので、この退行はレビューを生き延びる"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/shadow-cljs.edn"
       :find ":asset-path \"js\""
       :replace ":asset-path \"/js\""
       :must-fail [":wiring/asset-path-must-be-relative"]}

      {:id :wiring/module-is-renamed
       :why "module key を変えると shadow-cljs は素直に public/js/<新名>.js を出すが、文書は js/app.js を要求し続ける。compile は緑、テストも緑、ブラウザだけが 404 する"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/shadow-cljs.edn"
       :find ":modules {:app {:init-fn"
       :replace ":modules {:main {:init-fn"
       :must-fail [":wiring/bundle-src-mismatch"]}

      {:id :wiring/output-dir-moves-out-from-under-the-document
       :why "出力先を移すと、文書からの相対解決が外れる。どちらのファイルも単体では正しいままなので、突き合わせないと分からない"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/shadow-cljs.edn"
       :find ":output-dir \"public/js\""
       :replace ":output-dir \"dist/js\""
       :must-fail [":wiring/output-dir-not-under-document"]}

      {:id :wiring/init-fn-points-at-a-namespace-with-no-source
       :why ":init-fn が実在しない ns を指す形。shadow-cljs のビルドは落ちるが、ビルドを回さない経路（この gate 自身を含む）は committed なファイルだけで先に気付けた方がよい"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/shadow-cljs.edn"
       :find ":init-fn tenki.app/main"
       :replace ":init-fn tenki.core/main"
       :must-fail [":wiring/init-fn-namespace-has-no-source-file"]}

      {:id :wiring/mount-id-is-renamed-on-one-side
       :why "React の慣習に合わせて id を root にする、のような普通の変更。render が null に描くので画面は loading… のまま止まるが、build も cljs.test suite も DOM に触らないので両方緑のままになる"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/src/tenki/app.cljs"
       :find "getElementById \"app\""
       :replace "getElementById \"root\""
       :must-fail [":wiring/mount-id-absent-from-document"]}

      {:id :wiring/npm-test-runs-a-previous-build
       :why "**この suite で一番重い 1 件。** :output-to を変えると `npm test` は新しいファイルをコンパイルしてから out/ に残っている**前のビルドの成果物**を走らせる。緑になるが、それは変更前のコードに対する緑である。CLAUDE.md の『測れなかった検査が測って問題が無かった検査と同じ値を返す』そのもの"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/shadow-cljs.edn"
       :find ":output-to \"out/tests.js\""
       :replace ":output-to \"out/test.js\""
       :must-fail [":wiring/npm-test-does-not-run-shadow-test-output"]}

      {:id :wiring/ns-regexp-stops-matching-the-tests
       :why ":ns-regexp はどの名前空間を test bundle に**コンパイルするか**を決める。合わないファイルは失敗するテストではなく、テストですらない——suite はいつもの Ran N tests / 0 failures を出し、そのファイルは一度も実行されない"
       :file "appview/tenki-weather-component/cljs/shadow-cljs.edn"
       :find ":ns-regexp \"-test$\""
       :replace ":ns-regexp \"-spec$\""
       :must-fail [":wiring/test-ns-not-matched-by-ns-regexp"]}

      {:id :wiring/shadow-config-silently-closes-early
